\[
\begin{aligned}
  f\colon A\to\mathbb{R},\\
  f'\colon A\to\mathbb{R}.
\end{aligned}
\]

The existence of a Carath\'{e}odory factorization at $c$ is equivalent to $\operatorname{IsDifferentiable}(f,c,\mathbb{R},\mathbb{R})$ by the characterization theorem below. The factorizing function is explicit:
\[
\begin{aligned}
  f'\colon A\to\mathbb{R},\\
  f\colon A\to\mathbb{R},\\
\phi(x) =
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{f(x)-f(c)}{x-c} & x \neq c, \\[6pt]
f'(c) & x = c.
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
\]
Differentiability of $f$ at $c$ is precisely the statement that this piecewise-defined $\phi$ is continuous at $c$.

